    "staging/sm/Iterator/from/wrap-next-not-object-throws.js",
    "staging/sm/Map/iterable.js",
    "staging/sm/Symbol/keyFor.js",
    "staging/sm/module/module-export-name-star.js",

    // === PERMANENT EXCLUSIONS ===
    //
    // Everything above this banner is debt: a gap somebody is expected to pay down, each entry saying
    // what removes it. The entries below are not debt. Each one records a decision that Jint will not
    // implement the behaviour the test asserts, because no clause of the specification requires that
    // behaviour of Jint - the test pins an optional extension, or a result the spec deliberately
    // leaves implementation-defined. A pull request that wants to reverse one of these is welcome, but
    // it argues the decision and rewrites the reasoning; it does not just delete the line, because the
    // reason the line is here is not "nobody got round to it".

    // Legacy Function.prototype.caller / arguments.callee.caller: DECLINED.
    //
    // These are not the restricted accessors of AddRestrictedFunctionProperties
    // (https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-addrestrictedfunctionproperties), which Jint does implement:
    // %ThrowTypeError% sits on Function.prototype's "caller" and "arguments", so a strict function's
    // f.caller throws exactly as the spec requires. What these tests want is the separate legacy web
    // extension that hands back the *calling function* in sloppy mode, and nothing requires that of
    // anyone. Clause 17 (https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-error-handling-and-language-extensions) permits
    // it - "a host or implementation may provide additional types, values, objects, properties, and
    // functions beyond those described in this specification" - and 17.1
    // Forbidden Extensions (https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-forbidden-extensions) then only constrains an
    // implementation that takes the offer up (the value of such a "caller" property "must not be a
    // strict function object"). The stable suite treats it as optional and probes for it:
    // language/arguments-object/10.6-13-a-2.js and -3.js read arguments.callee.caller and take an
    // "Extension not supported - fake it" branch when it is undefined. Jint answers undefined for
    // exactly that reason (ScriptFunction.MaterializeCallerDescriptor), and that one value is what
    // every file below fails on.
    //
    // The reason to decline is isolation, not effort. Jint runs in-process inside host applications,
    // and .caller lets a script walk the stack and take a reference to a function it was never handed
    // - including a host-supplied callback, and through it whatever that callback closed over. That is
    // a sandboxing regression bought with a non-standard extension: a trade a browser makes and an
    // embedded engine should not.
    //
    // The shape of the debt, if the decision is ever reversed: 10 of the 12 want only f.caller. Two of
    // them additionally want f.arguments to be the running call's live arguments object -
    // extensions/function-properties.js and extensions/arguments-property-access-in-function.js -
    // which costs more than it looks, because Jint elides the arguments object entirely for a function
    // whose body never mentions `arguments` (JintFunctionDefinition's State.ArgumentsObjectNeeded) and
    // a constant-arity call site hands its arguments to an interpreted callee in registers without
    // building a JsValue[] at all (#2874).
    "staging/sm/Function/function-caller-restrictions.js",
    "staging/sm/extensions/arguments-property-access-in-function.js",
    "staging/sm/extensions/censor-strict-caller.js",
    "staging/sm/extensions/function-caller-skips-eval-frames.js",
    "staging/sm/extensions/function-properties.js",
    "staging/sm/regress/regress-577648-1.js",
    "staging/sm/regress/regress-577648-2.js",
    "staging/sm/regress/regress-584355.js",
    "staging/sm/regress/regress-586482-1.js",
    "staging/sm/regress/regress-586482-2.js",
    "staging/sm/regress/regress-586482-3.js",
    "staging/sm/regress/regress-586482-4.js",

    // Non-ISO Date parsing: DECLINED, and the weaker of the two decisions under this banner.
    //
    // Both files assert a heuristic fallback parser for strings that do not conform to the Date Time
    // String Format: a space where the "T" belongs, one-digit month/day/hour fields, an hours-only UTC
    // offset, a written month name in any of three positions, and two-digit years mapped onto
    // 1950-2049. Date.parse (https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-date.parse) puts all of that out of scope:
    // "If the String does not conform to that format the function may fall back to any
    // implementation-specific heuristics or implementation-specific date formats", and "the value
    // produced by this function is implementation-defined when given any String value that does not
    // conform to the Date Time String Format". The tests say as much of themselves - "For the sake of
    // cross compatibility with other implementations we implement date parsing heuristics". So nothing
    // here is required of Jint, and passing these files means writing a parser whose only
    // specification is another engine's source.
    //
    // What weakens that, stated plainly because it was measured rather than assumed: V8 passes both
    // files unmodified, so these are shared web behaviour rather than Mozilla quirks, and Jint's own
    // fallback diverges from both in ways that look more like accidents than choices - it reads
    // "1997-03-08 11" as 1997-03-08 rather than NaN (a trailing token is silently dropped), and it
    // reads a two-digit-year slash date as d/m/y ("1/2/1" is 1 February) while reading the
    // four-digit-year form as m/d/y, which is what V8 does for both. Wanting those tightened is
    // reasonable. It is still a change to implementation-defined behaviour and not a conformance fix,
    // which is why it sits here rather than in the debt list above - but of everything under this
    // banner it is the entry most likely to be argued back out, and a PR that argues it is welcome.
    "staging/sm/Date/non-iso.js",
    "staging/sm/Date/two-digit-years.js",

    // === STALE TEST ===

    // Written in 2017 against an edition of FunctionDeclarationInstantiation this test quotes in its
    // own info block - "22.f. Append \"arguments\" to parameterNames" - and superseded by the
    // normative change that split paramBindings out of paramNames. Today the Annex B condition reads
    // "paramNames does not contain funcName", so a block-level `function arguments(){}` IS eligible;
    // only the creation of a NEW var binding is skipped for that name, while the assignment made when
    // the declaration is evaluated still runs. V8 and SpiderMonkey both answer "function" where this
    // test demands the arguments object, and staging/sm/lexical-environment/
    // block-scoped-functions-annex-b-arguments.js in this very suite asserts the opposite of it.
    // Reported as tc39/test262#5113; tc39/test262#5112 corrects the file. Removed when that lands.
    "annexB/language/function-code/block-decl-func-skip-arguments.js",

